A||||10||fiero56||Fuel lines||||5||
Z||000000||fiero56||12-09-1999||11:12 PM||||I need to reconnect the fuel lines I disconnected during engine removal. I lost the diagram I made when I pulled the flexible lines from the steel ones. They connect on the firewall side near the battery. There are two of them. One comes from the fuel filter, and the other appears to come from somewhere near the gas tank. I need to know which flexible line connects to which steel line. This is for the v-6. Anybody know what I'm talking about?||||reg||5
Z||000001||fierospeeder||12-10-1999||12:33 AM||||the biggest steel line aka diameter, is the one that goes into the fuel filter<BR>the smaller one is the return into the fuel tank<BR>i think the big one is 3/8<BR>the smaller might be 5/16<BR>see if the threads fit into the fuel filter if you still cant get it||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||12-10-1999||07:24 AM||||Actually,<BR>The lines are connected at the bottom(fuel filter, gas tank),but I disconnected them near one of the heads. I believe the connection is just about level with the battery tray.  You will see two steel lines coming from around the plenum, I believe. Then they screw into these flexible lines that have a plastic housing with "ridges". <P>Hope this helps, I can post a picture later in the day if it stops raining here as to exactly which lines I need to connect.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||12-10-1999||06:14 PM||||Anybody?<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000004||william_thorniii||12-10-1999||08:00 PM||||I have been off of the forum for the last two days.  I will gladly check out my 85 GT tonight and see if I can decipher which one goes where.  Although you should be able to tell which is which by which fitting is larger.  Otherwise, can't you follow which goes to the rail?  Anyway, I will try to remember to check them when I go home from work tonight (probably around midnight EST)<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000005||fiero56||12-10-1999||10:07 PM||||Hey thanks for the offer William, but, I got them connected.  Took a little guess work, but I'm pretty sure I got the right hoses in the right spot.  Hopefully I'll find out tomorrow when I start her up for the first time in several weeks. All I have to say is, that clutch and everything else better work <B> perfectly </B>, cause I'm not doing all that work over again for quite a while.  <BR>I do have another question for the forum though.  Is there a break in procedure or period for a new clutch?  The flywheel is original, all I replaced was the clutch disk and pressure plate. Anything special to do?||||reg||7
Z||000006||batboy||12-11-1999||12:27 AM||||If you did not turn (resurface) the flywheel, just go easy on the clutch for a little while until the new clutch wears down enough to match all the micro grooves on the old flywheel. It really shouldn't take to long, assuming the flywheel was still relatively smooth (free of obvious, visible scores or grooves).||||reg||1
Z||000007||fiero56||12-11-1999||08:50 AM||||Flywheel wasn't touched.  Ed Parks advised, either leave it alone, or if it is bad enough to need resurfacing, replace it. He says, the shops that do that work, don't pay enough attention to how much they take off, and they usually take off too much.  <BR>The flywheel had no visible grooves. Just a few of those little heat bubble looking things. Just take it easy for a bit, sounds good to me. Thanks batboy.||||reg||1
Z||000008||batboy||12-11-1999||09:12 AM||||Ed is "the man" and knows what he's talking about. With a Fiero, if you turn the flywheel you're supposed to shim it out the amount it was turned, but few shops do this. You'll be ok. Sounds like it was done right.||||reg||1
Z||000009||william_thorniii||12-11-1999||09:56 PM||||Agreed... Ed's word is only slightly less holy than the Bible itself.  I would go easy on it though for at least the first couple hundred miles.  Assuming that your fly isn't too bad they should reseat well, but I always like to play it safe with something like that. <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000010||fiero56||12-11-1999||10:54 PM||||Thanks guys.  I was just wondering if there was any heat cycle it had to go through first.  Never replaced a clutch before. I always take it easy on my car. But I'll be sure I'm extra easy on it for a while.||||reg||10
